claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> This builds the hub that "[PATCH v2] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec
> the list's mail hub" [1] describes, on b/dev. It turns the relay jail
> into the list's home in steps, one service at a time. Each step
> leaves a jail that works and does what its README.org says.
>
> How mail moves, end to end, once the series is applied:
>
>   client --SMTP--> OpenSMTPD --LMTP--> Dovecot --> INBOX
>   Dovecot <--IMAP--> mbsync <--IMAPS--> Fastmail
>     (INBOX, Next, Later, and Archive only)

I want to take a step back. No new patches this round. Just rethinking
the design.

Do we need OpenSMTPD here? If LMTP can deliver messages directly - can
we call it directly, and take OpenSMTPD out of the mix?

Or should we treat LMTP as basically an optimization, but the initial
SMTP server should be full featured.

Basically I want to know what value each component brings.

Also doesn't OpenSMTPD have local delivery? Why can't it just deliver
straight to maildir?
